# Line endings are declared here, once, for everyone.
#
# Git will happily let two people end up with different bytes for the same file,
# and the result is a diff where every line changed and nothing did. That is the
# single most common way a repository rots, and it is entirely preventable: this
# file wins over every clone-local setting, so a contributor on Windows and one on
# Linux get the same working tree.
#
# The default is `eol=lf`. Every text file in this repository is LF in the object
# database and LF in the working tree, and nothing rewrites them. If you are on
# Windows and your editor is configured to save CRLF, fix the editor -- do not add
# a second rule here to cope with it.
#
# `text=auto` means Git decides what is text by inspecting the file rather than by
# trusting its extension, so a file we have not seen before still gets normalised.
# The explicit `binary` rules below then pin the ones we know about, so a stray
# detection failure cannot turn a PNG into a diff.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The exceptions, and each one is a real breakage rather than a preference.
#
# cmd.exe does not reliably handle LF in a batch file: a LF-terminated `goto`
# falls through into the next line and the script misbehaves in a way that looks
# like a logic error. CRLF in the working tree, LF in history.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# PowerShell tolerates LF, but Windows PowerShell 5.1 -- still the default on a
# fair number of machines -- has a long history of misreading LF-only scripts
# when they are read by means other than the parser. These are developer scripts,
# they are never compiled or diffed against generated output, and CRLF costs
# nothing.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Generated by `scripts/gen-brands.mjs` from a vendored Simple Icons package, and
# checked in on purpose so the build does not need Node. Marked linguist-generated
# so GitHub does not offer it for review or count it in language statistics -- a
# reviewer's attention is worth more than a green language bar.
apps/desktop/src/brands.js linguist-generated=true

# Binary assets. Tauri needs icons for a bundle; they are added when the shell is
# finished, and this rule is here so that day does not produce a corrupted file.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.exe binary
*.msi binary
*.nupkg binary

# Lockfiles are diffed by humans on purpose, and a merge conflict in Cargo.lock is
# resolved by taking either side and re-running the build. Never mark these
# binary to hide the conflict -- hide nothing. (This is the documented way to say
# "do not do the thing above", and it is here because `* binary` above is the
# habit it is meant to interrupt.)
Cargo.lock -diff linguist-generated=true

# Diffs we want to be readable when they happen. A queue file is small, plain
# text, and full of the URLs a user would want to see changed.
*.json text diff=json
*.m3u8 text
*.md text